---
import ExerciseLayout from '../layouts/ExerciseLayout.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../data/links';
import { estimateAll } from '../lib/onerepmax.js';
const chartWeights = [225,275,315,405,495];
const chartReps = [2, 3, 5];
const chartRows = chartWeights.map((weight) => ({ weight, cells: chartReps.map((r) => estimateAll(weight, r).average) }));
---
<ExerciseLayout
  title="Deadlift One Rep Max Calculator — Estimate Your Deadlift 1RM"
  description="Estimate your deadlift one rep max from a set of 1–12 reps. Six formulas, why estimates may read low for the deadlift, and loadable training weights."
  path="/deadlift-calculator/"
  name="Deadlift Calculator"
  h1="Deadlift One Rep Max (1RM) Calculator"
  lead="Estimate your deadlift max from a recent heavy set. Research suggests formula estimates tend to run low on this lift — here is what that means for your number."
  exercise="deadlift"
  lift="Deadlift"
  example={{ weight: 315, reps: 3, unit: 'lb' }}
  related={[LINKS.squat, LINKS.row, LINKS.dots, LINKS.test]}
  sources={['lesuer', 'reynolds', 'epley', 'hprc']}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="dl-estimate-heading">
    <h2 id="dl-estimate-heading">How to estimate your deadlift max</h2>
    <p class="answer">
      Pull a weight you can lift for 2–5 clean reps, resetting each rep from the floor, and enter
      the weight and reps above. For example, 315 lb × 3 estimates a deadlift max of roughly
      334–359 lb — though your true max may be higher, because formulas have been found to
      underestimate the deadlift.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dl-range-heading">
    <h2 id="dl-range-heading">Best rep range for a deadlift max estimate</h2>
    <p>
      Use a set of <strong>2–5 reps</strong>. Estimates are generally closer to a tested max when the
      set is heavy and short: in a study using the chest press and leg press, a 5-rep max predicted the 1RM more accurately than a 10- or
      20-rep max (Reynolds et al., 2006). Long deadlift sets also tend to end from grip or lower-back
      fatigue, which makes the estimate read even lower.
    </p>
    <h3>Deadlift 1RM chart</h3>
    <p>
      Find the weight you pulled and the reps you got. Each number is the Average Estimate of the six
      formulas, rounded to the nearest pound. The calculator above also works in kg and shows the full range.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Estimated deadlift 1RM (Average Estimate, lb) by weight and reps</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Weight lifted</th>{chartReps.map((r) => <th scope="col" class="num">{r} reps</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {chartRows.map((row) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{row.weight} lb</th>{row.cells.map((v) => <td class="num">{v.toFixed(0)} lb</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dl-low-heading">
    <h2 id="dl-low-heading">Why deadlift estimates may read low</h2>
    <p>
      In a study that compared seven published 1RM equations against tested maxes in the bench
      press, squat and deadlift, <strong>every equation significantly underestimated the deadlift</strong>
      (LeSuer et al., 1997). The study tested untrained college students, so the size of the gap
      for experienced lifters is not known — but the direction is worth knowing.
    </p>
    <p>
      A plausible explanation (our reasoning, not a finding of that study): on high-rep deadlift sets, grip and lower-back fatigue often end the set
      before the legs and hips are truly exhausted. A single does not suffer from that fatigue, so
      people can often lift more than a long set predicts. Lower-rep sets (2–5) reduce the problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      Treat the deadlift estimate as a conservative guide. The top of the range, or a little above
      the Average Estimate, may be closer to your real max — but do not program from an optimistic number.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dl-rep-heading">
    <h2 id="dl-rep-heading">What counts as a clean deadlift rep</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Dead-stop reps give the most honest estimate.</strong> Bouncing the plates off the floor (touch-and-go) uses rebound and makes the set easier.</li>
      <li><strong>Lockout counts:</strong> stand fully upright with hips and knees locked. No hitching the bar up the thighs.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the same grip, stance (conventional or sumo) and equipment</strong> as the max you want to predict. Straps help grip on high-rep sets, so an estimate from strapped reps may not match an unstrapped max.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dl-safety-heading">
    <h2 id="dl-safety-heading">Deadlift safety for heavy sets</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Stop the set when your back position changes noticeably — a rep with poor position is not worth the estimate.</li>
      <li>Use plates of standard diameter so the bar starts at a consistent height.</li>
      <li>Leave a rep or two in reserve on high-rep sets; deadlift fatigue accumulates quickly.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="dl-faq-heading">
    <h2 id="dl-faq-heading">Deadlift max: common questions</h2>
    <h3>How do I calculate my deadlift 1RM by hand?</h3>
    <p>
      Use the Epley formula: <strong>1RM = weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30)</strong>. For 315 lb × 3:
      315 × (1 + 3 ÷ 30) = 315 × 1.1 = <strong>346.5 lb</strong>. The calculator runs five more
      formulas and shows the range.
    </p>
    <h3>Does sumo or conventional change the estimate?</h3>
    <p>
      The formulas do not know your stance, so the estimate only applies to the stance you used for
      the set. Many lifters are stronger in one style than the other, so do not use a sumo set to
      predict a conventional max, or the other way round.
    </p>
    <h3>Should I test or estimate my deadlift max?</h3>
    <p>
      For planning training, an estimate from a hard set of 2–5 reps is usually enough and is much
      less tiring than a true max. If you need an exact number, for example before a competition,
      follow a structured protocol such as <a href="/guides/how-to-test-1rm/">how to test your 1RM safely</a>.
    </p>
    <h3>How often should I update my deadlift max?</h3>
    <p>
      Whenever you complete a new hard set in the 2–5 rep range. For many lifters that is every few
      weeks, or at the end of each training block.
    </p>
  </section>
</ExerciseLayout>
